          lemma: "τίνος υἱός ἐστιν; … τῷ Κυρίῳ μου",
          title: "David's son, David's Lord — the question that ended the questions",
          body: "After a day of ambushes — tax trap, resurrection trap, commandment test — Jesus asks the examiners one question of his own, and it is not a trap but the deepest question in the book, the one every interrogation had been circling: *what do you think about the Christ — whose son is he?* Their answer is orthodox and safe: **David's** — Messiah as royal descendant, the nation's best warrior-king upgraded. Jesus neither denies it (his own gospel opens ”son of David,” and blind men have shouted it for chapters) nor lets it stand as sufficient. He quotes **Psalm 110:1** — in the Hebrew, *YHWH said to my adon*: David, the psalm's speaker, calls the Messiah **my Lord**, and no patriarch calls his own descendant ”lord”; ancestry runs the honour the other way. So: *if David calls him Lord, how is he his son?* The question has an answer — this gospel has been spelling it since the virgin's son was named Emmanuel: David's son by lineage, David's Lord by an origin higher than lineage — but Jesus does not supply it. He leaves the paradox standing in the temple court, and the silence that follows is the chapter's verdict: **no one could answer him a word, nor did anyone dare question him any more.** Psalm 110:1 became the early church's most-quoted Old Testament verse — the risen Lord ”at the right hand” — which is to say the church's whole creed grew from the gap this question opened. Categories that measure Messiah by pedigree alone were always one size too small; the interrogations end because the interrogators glimpse, for a moment, what would actually be required to answer.",
